/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE FEED IS AN ANALYSIS, written the way an analysis comes.

  A laboratory does not report a water as "calcium carbonate plus carbon
  dioxide".  It reports calcium and alkalinity -- the IONS it measured.  Every
  other reactive case in this corpus makes the author convert that into
  components first, and that conversion is not innocent: it is a choice of
  LABELS.  The same ions can be formulated as several different component sets
  (NaCl + KBr and NaBr + KCl are the textbook pair), and picking one silently
  is exactly what the basis-rank test exists to stop.

  So this stream declares its own basis and hands the engine the measurement.

  WHAT THE ENGINE DOES WITH IT.  m = A n, solved for n against the components'
  DECLARED bridges -- CO2 -> HCO3, CaCO3 -> Ca + HCO3 (the H leg is the
  network's shared mediator and is never a master).  Two masters, two
  components, rank 2:

      Ca    = 0.0122          ->   CaCO3 = 0.0122 kmol/h
      HCO3  = 0.0244          ->   CO2   = 0.0244 - 0.0122 = 0.0122 kmol/h

  Not a conversion the author did in their head and typed in: an inversion the
  engine performed, and would have REFUSED to perform if it were not unique.

  WHY THE NUMBERS BALANCE.  `basis analytical;` says this is a measured water,
  and a measured water closes in charge: 2 x 0.0122 of Ca(2+) against 0.0244
  of HCO3(-) is exactly zero.  The engine checks it and refuses an analysis
  that does not -- an unbalanced water is a measurement to fix, never a
  residue for the solved pH to absorb.  A `stoichiometric` set is the other
  case and does NOT balance, because H/OH are excluded mediators; declaring
  which one you have is therefore not bookkeeping, it decides the check.

  This is a calcium-bicarbonate water -- the commonest natural water there is.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
speciesMolarFlows
{
    network   carbonate;         // WHICH chemistry set these names belong to
    basis     analytical;        // a measured water: charge MUST close

    Ca         0.0122  kmol/h;   // calcium
    HCO3       0.0244  kmol/h;   // alkalinity, as bicarbonate
    water     97.0     kmol/h;
}
T               313.15 K;
P               101325 Pa;
